# Credit Scoring on LendingClub Loans (SQL + Python)

An end-to-end individual credit scoring project built on the public LendingClub dataset: sample construction and feature engineering in SQL (DuckDB), a logistic regression baseline, an XGBoost challenger with SHAP explanations, out-of-time validation (AUC / KS / PSI), and a cutoff analysis translated into business terms.

## Highlights

- **Out-of-time validation:** trained on loans issued in 2013-2014, tested on 2015, which mimics how a model is used in practice.
- **Leakage control:** only variables known at application time are used, selected with a whitelist rather than a blacklist. Post-origination fields such as `total_pymnt`, `recoveries`, `last_pymnt_d` and `out_prncp` can therefore never enter the model.
- **No lender-assigned risk signals:** `grade`, `sub_grade`, `int_rate` and `installment` are excluded on purpose. They are outputs of LendingClub's own underwriting model, so including them would make this model largely reproduce the lender's decision instead of assessing risk from borrower information.
- **Interpretable baseline first:** a logistic regression is the reference model; XGBoost is compared against it and explained with SHAP.
- **Honest limitations:** the model underestimates the 2015 default rate even though score PSI is very low (see Business findings).

## Results at a glance

Test set: 283,026 loans issued in 2015 (actual default rate 14.89%).

| Model | AUC | KS |
|---|---|---|
| Logistic regression (32 variables) | 0.659 | 0.230 |
| XGBoost (34 variables) | 0.672 | 0.250 |

Score PSI between train and test is 0.003 (logistic regression) and 0.002 (XGBoost), i.e. very stable.

## Data and sample

**Source.** LendingClub accepted loans, 2007-2018Q4 (`accepted_2007_to_2018Q4.csv`, 2,260,701 rows, 151 columns). Only the accepted-loans file is used. The data is not included in this repository; download it from Kaggle: [wordsforthewise/lending-club](https://www.kaggle.com/wordsforthewise/lending-club) and place the file under `data/raw/`.

**Compliance note.** This project uses only a publicly available, anonymised dataset. No proprietary data and no personally identifiable customer information are used.

**Sample definition.**

- Loan status is `Fully Paid` or `Charged Off` (loans with a final outcome only).
- Term of 36 months.
- Issued between 2013 and 2015.
- Result: **546,018 loans**.

**Target.** `default_flag = 1` if the loan status is `Charged Off`, otherwise 0.

**Time-based split.**

| Period | Role | Loans | Defaults | Default rate |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2013 | train | 100,422 | 12,378 | 12.33% |
| 2014 | train | 162,570 | 22,315 | 13.73% |
| 2015 | test | 283,026 | 42,132 | 14.89% |

Train set: 262,992 loans (default rate 13.19%). The default rate rises year over year, so the test period is a genuinely shifted population.

## Methodology

### 1. Cleaning and sample construction in SQL (DuckDB)

- The 1.6 GB CSV is converted to Parquet once. All columns are read as text first and converted with `TRY_CAST`, so malformed values become `NULL` instead of stopping the job.
- Variables are chosen with a **whitelist**: only columns explicitly justified as known at application time are selected. A column that is not named is never used, so a leaky or unfamiliar field cannot slip in by accident.
- Formats are standardised: `term` is trimmed, `issue_d` becomes a date, `emp_length` becomes years, `revol_util` loses its `%`, `fico` is the midpoint of the low/high range, and `credit_hist_months` is the gap between the earliest credit line and the issue date.
- Output: `loans_clean.parquet` (546,018 rows, 26 columns: identifiers and labels plus 21 candidate features). The full query is in [`sql/01_clean.sql`](sql/01_clean.sql).

### 2. Exploration and preprocessing

- Univariate analysis (default rate by bin) confirmed that the labels and the direction of key variables make sense, for example default rate falls from 17.0% to 4.4% as FICO rises.
- All preprocessing parameters are **learned on the training set only** and then applied unchanged to the test set:
  - Winsorisation: 1st to 99th percentile for `annual_inc`, `dti`, `revol_bal`; an upper cap at the 99.9th percentile for `revol_util`, `open_acc`, `total_acc`, `credit_hist_months`.
  - Missing values: median imputation plus a missing-indicator column. The indicator matters: loans with no reported employment length default at 19.4% versus 12.8% for the rest of the training set.
  - Count variables (`delinq_2yrs`, `pub_rec`, `pub_rec_bankruptcies`) are capped at 2.
  - Rare categories are merged, categorical variables are one-hot encoded, and variables are standardised for the logistic regression only.
- The learned parameters are saved in `reports/preprocessing_rules.json` so the same treatment can be applied to new loans.
- `addr_state` is not used in the models. Geography can act as a proxy for sensitive characteristics, its signal may not be stable over time, and it adds dozens of categories.

### 3. Models

- **Logistic regression (baseline).** 32 variables. Two redundant variables were removed after a correlation check (`has_public_record` correlates 0.95 with `pub_rec`; `open_acc` overlaps with `total_acc`), at a cost of 0.002 AUC.
- **XGBoost (challenger).** 34 variables, unscaled. Hyperparameters are fixed and conservative (300 trees, depth 4, learning rate 0.05, row and column subsampling of 0.8, minimum child weight 50) and were not tuned on the test set. Explained with SHAP on a random sample of 5,000 test loans.

### 4. Evaluation

- **AUC and KS** on both train and test sets.
- **PSI** of the model score and of each feature between train and test.
- **Cutoff analysis:** score cutoffs are set from training-score quantiles and applied unchanged to 2015 loans, reporting approval rate, bad rate among approved loans, and the share of defaulters screened out.

## Results

### Discrimination (test set: 2015 loans)

| Model | Variables | AUC | KS |
|---|---|---|---|
| Logistic regression (baseline) | 32 | 0.659 | 0.230 |
| XGBoost | 34 | 0.672 | 0.250 |

- The AUC values sit in the range expected when only application-time information is used and the lender's own grade and interest rate are deliberately left out.
- No sign of overfitting or leakage: train versus test AUC is 0.684 vs 0.672 for XGBoost and 0.656 vs 0.660 for the first logistic regression (34 variables, before removing the two redundant ones).
- XGBoost improves on the logistic regression by about 0.013 AUC. A small gain is consistent with the SHAP analysis below: both models rely on the same variables in the same direction.

![ROC curves](reports/figures/roc_comparison.png)

### What drives the score

A single-variable view already shows a clear gradient. Default rate in the training set falls from 17.0% to 4.4% across FICO bands:

![Default rate by FICO band](reports/figures/default_rate_by_fico.png)

SHAP on 5,000 randomly sampled test loans ranks the drivers of the XGBoost model (mean absolute SHAP value in parentheses): FICO (0.30), annual income (0.20), debt-to-income ratio (0.15), loan amount (0.12) and credit inquiries in the last 6 months (0.12). The ranking and the signs are close to the logistic regression coefficients.

![SHAP summary (bar)](reports/figures/shap_bar.png)

![SHAP summary (beeswarm)](reports/figures/shap_beeswarm.png)

- **Lower risk:** higher FICO, higher income, more total accounts, longer credit history, credit-card purpose.
- **Higher risk:** higher DTI, larger loan, more recent inquiries, renting, higher revolving utilisation, and a missing employment length.

Two findings that look counter-intuitive at first:

- Loan amount appears protective in a single-variable view but raises risk in both models. This most likely reflects that larger loans go to higher-income borrowers; once income is held constant the sign flips. This explanation was not tested separately.
- Loans with a verified income default more often than unverified ones. A plausible reason is selection: the lender may verify the applicants it considers riskier. This was also not tested.

### Stability between training and test periods

| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| Score PSI, logistic regression | 0.003 |
| Score PSI, XGBoost | 0.002 |
| Largest feature PSI | `initial_list_status_w`: 0.142 |
| Next largest feature PSI | `verification_status_Source Verified`: 0.054 |
| All remaining features | below 0.05, most below 0.01 |

By the usual rule of thumb (below 0.1 stable, 0.1 to 0.25 worth watching), the score distribution is very stable. The only variable above 0.1, `initial_list_status`, describes how the platform listed the loan rather than the borrower, and it has almost no weight in either model.

However, stable inputs do not mean accurate levels. The 2015 default rate is higher than the training rate, and both models underestimate it:

| | Train | Test (2015) |
|---|---|---|
| Actual default rate | 13.19% | 14.89% |
| Mean predicted default probability, logistic regression | 13.19% | 13.21% |
| Mean predicted default probability, XGBoost | 13.19% | 13.38% |

The ranking of borrowers still holds up (AUC 0.67), but predicted probabilities should not be read as absolute default rates. The cause of the rise was not investigated.

## Business findings

A score only becomes a decision once a cutoff is chosen. Cutoffs below are taken from training-score quantiles and applied unchanged to 2015 loans using the XGBoost score. Without any cutoff, the bad rate on the 2015 test set is 14.89%.

| Target approval rate (train) | Approval rate (2015) | Bad rate, approved | Bad rate, rejected | Defaulters screened out |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 90% | 88.9% | 13.1% | 29.3% | 21.7% |
| 80% | 78.8% | 11.8% | 26.3% | 37.5% |
| 70% | 68.8% | 10.7% | 24.1% | 50.6% |
| 60% | 59.0% | 9.6% | 22.5% | 61.9% |
| 50% | 49.2% | 8.6% | 21.0% | 71.7% |
| 40% | 39.5% | 7.5% | 19.7% | 80.2% |
| 30% | 29.7% | 6.4% | 18.5% | 87.2% |

![Approval rate vs bad rate](reports/figures/approval_vs_badrate.png)

**Findings**

1. **A moderate cutoff already removes a lot of risk.** Approving about 69% of applicants lowers the bad rate among approved loans from 14.9% to 10.7% (about 28% lower in relative terms) while screening out roughly half of all defaulters. The rejected group defaults at 24.1%, about 2.3 times the approved group.
2. **Returns diminish as the cutoff tightens.** The extra applicants rejected when moving from 90% to 80% approval default at 23.2%; from 80% to 70%, 19.5%; from 70% to 60%, 17.2%; from 60% to 50%, 14.8%; from 50% to 40%, 13.1%; and from 40% to 30%, 10.7%. By the 60%-to-50% step the marginal group defaults at about the portfolio average (14.9%), so tightening beyond that point rejects borrowers who are no worse than average.
3. **XGBoost beats the logistic regression in business terms, modestly.** At comparable approval rates its approved portfolio has a lower bad rate, by roughly 0.2 percentage points at loose cutoffs and up to about 0.7 at strict ones (read from the chart; the two models do not have identical approval rates). Given the small gap, the logistic regression remains a reasonable choice when interpretability and ease of review matter.
4. **There is no single "best" cutoff in this data.** Both curves fall almost linearly with no obvious kink. The best cutoff depends on how much a good loan earns and how much a default costs. This project excludes interest rates and recoveries on purpose, so it does not estimate that trade-off. The 70% row is an illustration, not a recommendation.
5. **Set cutoffs on observed bad rates, not predicted probabilities.** The models underestimate the 2015 default rate (see Results), so predicted probabilities are too low in absolute terms even though the ranking is reliable.
6. **Monitor after deployment.** Track score PSI, the gap between predicted and actual default rates, and the bad rate by score band. Low PSI alone would not have revealed the underestimation seen here.

## Limitations

- **Accepted loans only.** Applicants the platform rejected are not observed, so results describe the population LendingClub already approved. No reject inference was attempted.
- **Calibration drift.** Both models underestimate the 2015 default rate (13.2% to 13.4% predicted versus 14.9% actual). The cause was not investigated, so the cutoff analysis relies on observed bad rates.
- **No profit or loss data.** The optimal cutoff cannot be derived, only the trade-off between approval rate and bad rate.
- **Outcome selection.** Only loans with a final status (`Fully Paid` or `Charged Off`) are kept. In the 2015 test cohort this excludes 147 of 283,173 loans (0.05%) that were still current or late at the data snapshot. Even if every one of them had defaulted, the 2015 default rate would move only from 14.89% to about 14.93%, so the effect is negligible. Earlier cohorts were not checked.
- **Untested explanations.** The reasons given for the loan-amount sign change and for the higher default rate of verified-income loans are hypotheses.
- **Fixed hyperparameters.** XGBoost was not tuned, so its gain over the logistic regression should not be read as a ceiling.
- **Conservative variable choices.** `mort_acc` and `tot_cur_bal` were left out because the dataset is a late snapshot and it could not be confirmed that their values were known at application time. `addr_state` was left out for the reasons given in Methodology; adding it to XGBoost is a natural follow-up.
- **Scope.** One US peer-to-peer platform, one product (36-month personal loans), one three-year window. Data availability, credit bureau coverage, regulation and customer mix at a bank in another market differ, so the numbers do not transfer directly; the workflow (leakage control, out-of-time validation, PSI, cutoff analysis) does.

